        <!-- Bottom CTA -->
        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Mistakes Sealing New Asphalt
          </h2>

          <p>
            New asphalt is the one case where doing the maintenance early actively harms the surface, and it catches out a lot of new homeowners.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing in the First Season</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh asphalt needs six to twelve months to cure and release its volatile oils. Seal it inside that window and those oils are trapped, leaving pavement that stays soft, scuffs under tyres, and marks where anything stands on it. Let it get through a full summer first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mistaking Normal Curing for a Defect</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New asphalt can look slightly uneven in colour, feel soft in hot weather, and show tyre marks in its first months. That is the surface curing, not a failure, and sealing it to "fix" the appearance makes it permanent. Give it time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving and Parking Too Heavily Too Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Before it has cured, asphalt deforms under concentrated loads — kickstands, jack stands, trailer jacks, and parked wheels in the same spot all leave permanent dents. Vary where you park and put a board under any point load through the first season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting Too Long After the First Year</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The opposite error is treating the wait as indefinite. Once the asphalt has cured, UV and water start working on the binder, and an unsealed second and third summer costs real life. Seal once through the first cure, then settle into a two-to-four-year cycle.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A <strong class="text-white">new asphalt driveway</strong> should not be sealed in its first season. It needs six to twelve months to cure and off-gas, and sealing early traps those oils in a surface that then stays soft and marks easily.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Get through the first full summer, protect it from point loads in the meantime, and then seal once the surface has hardened. After that, settle into sealing every two to four years judged on condition — and always fill the cracks before the coat goes down.
